r.\n\n5. **If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.**.\n   - Enjoying dining hall meals implies spending a lot of time in the dining halls.\n\n6. **If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.**.\n   - This statement is contradictory. It says that if Mary works in student jobs and needs to earn money for tuition, then she is not picky and does not need to earn money for tuition. This is a paradoxical statement.\n\nGiven the last statement, we have a contradiction. If Mary works in student jobs on campus, she needs to earn money for tuition (from the first statement). However, the last statement says that if she works in student jobs and needs to earn money for tuition, then she does not need to earn money for tuition. This creates a logical inconsistency.\n\nTherefore, based on the given information, the statement "Mary needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition" is **uncertain** due to the contradictory nature of the last statement. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
